title: Mesopotamia
description: Historical region of West Asia
image: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/N-Mesopotamia_and_Syria_english.svg/3840px-N-Mesopotamia_and_Syria_english.svg.png
images:
  - file: Mosul_river.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Mosul_river.jpg/250px-Mosul_river.jpg
    width: 2000
    height: 1334
    caption: The Tigris river flowing through the region of modern Mosul in Upper
      Mesopotamia.
  - file: اهوار_العراق_ليلا.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/%D8%A7%D9%87%D9%88%D8%A7%D8%B1_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%82_%D9%84%D9%8A%D9%84%D8%A7.jpg/250px-%D8%A7%D9%87%D9%88%D8%A7%D8%B1_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%82_%D9%84%D9%8A%D9%84%D8%A7.jpg
    width: 5873
    height: 3952
    caption: Mesopotamian Marshes at night, southern Iraq. A reed house (Mudhif) and
      a narrow canoe (Mashoof) are in the water. Mudhif structures have been one
      of the traditional types of structures, built by the Marsh people of
      southern Mesopotamia for at least 5,000 years. A carved elevation of a
      typical mudhif, dating to around 3,300 BC was discovered at Uruk.
  - file: Statue_Gudea_Met_59.2.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Statue_Gudea_Met_59.2.jpg/250px-Statue_Gudea_Met_59.2.jpg
    width: 2687
    height: 4044
    caption: One of 27 Statues of Gudea, a ruler around 2090 BC
  - file: Jarmo_to_Ubaid_7000-4500.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Jarmo_to_Ubaid_7000-4500.jpg/500px-Jarmo_to_Ubaid_7000-4500.jpg
    width: 2125
    height: 1766
    caption: After early starts in Jarmo (red dot, c. 7500 BC), the civilization of
      Mesopotamia in the 7th–5th millennium BC was centered around the Hassuna
      culture in the north, the Halaf culture in the northwest, the Samarra
      culture in central Mesopotamia and the Ubaid culture in the southeast,
      which later expanded to encompass the whole region.
  - file: Ancient_Egypt_and_Mesopotamia_c._1450_BC.png
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Ancient_Egypt_and_Mesopotamia_c._1450_BC.png/250px-Ancient_Egypt_and_Mesopotamia_c._1450_BC.png
    width: 1200
    height: 1473
    caption: A map of 15th century BC, showing the core territory of Assyria with
      its two major cities Assur and Nineveh wedged between Babylonia
      downstream. The states of Mitanni and Hatti are upstream.
  - file: P1050763_Louvre_code_Hammurabi_face_rwk.JPG
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/P1050763_Louvre_code_Hammurabi_face_rwk.JPG/250px-P1050763_Louvre_code_Hammurabi_face_rwk.JPG
    width: 1215
    height: 2000
    caption: The Code of Hammurabi is a Babylonian legal text composed c. 1755–1750
      BC. It is the longest, best-organised, and best-preserved legal text from
      the ancient Near East. It is written in the Old Babylonian dialect of
      Akkadian, purportedly by Hammurabi, sixth king of the First Dynasty of
      Babylon.
  - file: Tablet_XI_or_the_Flood_Tablet_of_the_Epic_of_Gilgamesh,_currently_housed_in_the_British_Museum_in_London.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Tablet_XI_or_the_Flood_Tablet_of_the_Epic_of_Gilgamesh%2C_currently_housed_in_the_British_Museum_in_London.jpg/250px-Tablet_XI_or_the_Flood_Tablet_of_the_Epic_of_Gilgamesh%2C_currently_housed_in_the_British_Museum_in_London.jpg
    width: 6016
    height: 4016
    caption: The Epic of Gilgamesh, an epic poem from ancient Mesopotamia, regarded
      as the earliest surviving notable literature.
  - file: Clay_tablet,_mathematical,_geometric-algebraic,_similar_to_the_Euclidean_geometry._From_Tell_Harmal,_Iraq._2003-1595_BCE._Iraq_Museum.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Clay_tablet%2C_mathematical%2C_geometric-algebraic%2C_similar_to_the_Euclidean_geometry._From_Tell_Harmal%2C_Iraq._2003-1595_BCE._Iraq_Museum.jpg/250px-Clay_tablet%2C_mathematical%2C_geometric-algebraic%2C_similar_to_the_Euclidean_geometry._From_Tell_Harmal%2C_Iraq._2003-1595_BCE._Iraq_Museum.jpg
    width: 1499
    height: 2084
    caption: A clay tablet, mathematical, geometric-algebraic, similar to the
      Euclidean geometry. From Shaduppum Iraq. 2003–1595 BC. Iraq Museum.
  - file: Medical_recipe_concerning_poisoning._Terracotta_tablet,_from_Nippur,_Iraq,_18th_century_BCE._Ancient_Orient_Museum,_Istanbul.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Medical_recipe_concerning_poisoning._Terracotta_tablet%2C_from_Nippur%2C_Iraq%2C_18th_century_BCE._Ancient_Orient_Museum%2C_Istanbul.jpg/250px-Medical_recipe_concerning_poisoning._Terracotta_tablet%2C_from_Nippur%2C_Iraq%2C_18th_century_BCE._Ancient_Orient_Museum%2C_Istanbul.jpg
    width: 3379
    height: 4999
    caption: A medical recipe concerning poisoning. Terracotta tablet, from Nippur,
      Iraq.
  - file: Lilith_Periodo_de_Isin_Larsa_y_Babilonia.JPG
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Lilith_Periodo_de_Isin_Larsa_y_Babilonia.JPG/250px-Lilith_Periodo_de_Isin_Larsa_y_Babilonia.JPG
    width: 778
    height: 990
    caption: The Burney Relief, First Babylonian dynasty, around 1800 BC
  - file: Kudurru_Melishipak_Louvre_Sb23_n02.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Kudurru_Melishipak_Louvre_Sb23_n02.jpg/250px-Kudurru_Melishipak_Louvre_Sb23_n02.jpg
    width: 2913
    height: 2623
    caption: King Meli-shipak I (1186–1172 BC) presents his daughter to the goddess
      Nannaya. The crescent moon represents the god Sin, the sun the Shamash and
      the star the goddess Ishtar.
  - file: The_Queen's_gold_lyre_from_the_Royal_Cemetery_at_Ur._C._2500_BCE._Iraq_Museum.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/The_Queen%27s_gold_lyre_from_the_Royal_Cemetery_at_Ur._C._2500_BCE._Iraq_Museum.jpg/250px-The_Queen%27s_gold_lyre_from_the_Royal_Cemetery_at_Ur._C._2500_BCE._Iraq_Museum.jpg
    width: 3536
    height: 4016
    caption: The Queen's gold lyre from the Royal Cemetery at Ur. c. 2500 BC. Iraq
      Museum
  - file: Jemdet_Nasr_Cylinder_Seal_With_Hunting_Scene.png
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Jemdet_Nasr_Cylinder_Seal_With_Hunting_Scene.png/250px-Jemdet_Nasr_Cylinder_Seal_With_Hunting_Scene.png
    width: 1282
    height: 406
    caption: Jemdet Nasr Cylinder presenting a hunting scene, with two lions and an
      antelope. c. 3100 to 2900 BC.
  - file: Babylonian_marriage_market.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Babylonian_marriage_market.jpg/500px-Babylonian_marriage_market.jpg
    width: 3069
    height: 1720
    caption: The Babylonian marriage market by the 19th-century painter Edwin Long
  - file: Metal_production_in_Ancient_Middle_East.svg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Metal_production_in_Ancient_Middle_East.svg/330px-Metal_production_in_Ancient_Middle_East.svg.png
    width: 1249
    height: 693
    caption: Mining areas of the ancient West Asia.
  - file: Fertile_Crescent.svg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Fertile_Crescent.svg/250px-Fertile_Crescent.svg.png
    width: 294
    height: 345
    caption: A map of the Fertile Crescent including the location of ancient
      Mesopotamia between the Tigris and Euphrates rivers.
  - file: Ashurbanipal_in_a_chariot,_wall_relief,_7th_century_BC,_from_Nineveh,_the_British_Museum.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Ashurbanipal_in_a_chariot%2C_wall_relief%2C_7th_century_BC%2C_from_Nineveh%2C_the_British_Museum.jpg/250px-Ashurbanipal_in_a_chariot%2C_wall_relief%2C_7th_century_BC%2C_from_Nineveh%2C_the_British_Museum.jpg
    width: 6016
    height: 4016
    caption: A 7th-century BC relief depicting Ashurbanipal, r. 669–631 BC, and
      three royal attendants in a chariot.
  - file: Campaign_in_southern_Iraq_of_Ashurbanipal_-_fighting_in_the_marshes.jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Campaign_in_southern_Iraq_of_Ashurbanipal_-_fighting_in_the_marshes.jpg/250px-Campaign_in_southern_Iraq_of_Ashurbanipal_-_fighting_in_the_marshes.jpg
    width: 5720
    height: 4000
    caption: A relief showing a campaign in the Mesopotamian Marshes of southern
      Babylonia during the reign of Ashurbanipal. Assyrian soldiers are on a
      boat, chasing fleeing enemies. Some are hiding in the reeds
  - file: Denis_Bourez_-_British_Museum,_London_(8747049029)_(2).jpg
    src: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Denis_Bourez_-_British_Museum%2C_London_%288747049029%29_%282%29.jpg/250px-Denis_Bourez_-_British_Museum%2C_London_%288747049029%29_%282%29.jpg
    width: 4288
    height: 3216
    caption: The Standard of Ur, 2600 BC, the Early Dynastic Period III. Shell, red
      limestone and lapis lazuli on wood. Discovered at the Royal Cemetery at
      Ur, Dhi Qar Governorate, Iraq
