[[ ^66 ]]: Bryce, James (1886). [[ "The Relations of History and Geography" | https://books.google.com/books?id=YIEkAQAAIAAJ ]]. //Littell's Living Age//. 5. !!169!!. Boston: Littell and Company: 70. [[ Archived | https://web.archive.org/web/20210411224831/https://books.google.com/books?id=YIEkAQAAIAAJ ]] from the original on 11 April 2021. Retrieved 10 April 2021. There was also an important trade route through central Asia, which coming down through Persia and Mesopotamia to the Levant, reached the sea in northern Syria [\...]. These trade routes assumed enormous importance in the earlier Middle Ages, and upon them great political issues turned.

[[ ^67 ]]: [[ Bulliet, Richard | richard-bulliet ]]; [[ Crossley, Pamela Kyle | pamela-kyle-crossley ]]; [[ Headrick, Daniel R. | daniel-r.-headrick ]]; Hirsch, Steven W.; Johnson, Lyman L.; Northrup, David (2009). "Interregional Patterns of Culture and Contact". [[ //The Earth and Its Peoples: A Global History// | https://books.google.com/books?id=8kfAAgAAQBAJ ]] (6 ed.). Cengage Learning (published 2014). p. 279. ISBN [[ 978-1-305-14709-6 | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special%3ABookSources%2F978-1-305-14709-6 ]]. [[ Archived | https://web.archive.org/web/20210411224830/https://books.google.com/books?id=8kfAAgAAQBAJ ]] from the original on 11 April 2021. Retrieved 10 April 2021. Eurasia's overland trade faded, and merchants, soldiers, and explorers took to the seas.

